            <title>[LU-21137] TCU: rm fails with ENAMETOOLONG for names &gt;~225 bytes deleted repeatedly (timestamp suffix)</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-21137</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;h2&gt;&lt;a name="Problem"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Problem&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;tt&gt;mdd_trash_repeated_name_append()&lt;/tt&gt; (&lt;tt&gt;mdd_trash.c:623-667&lt;/tt&gt;) appends &lt;tt&gt;.YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS&lt;span class="error"&gt;&amp;#91;.nnnnnnnnn&amp;#93;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/tt&gt; (20–30 bytes) to the full name. When the result exceeds &lt;tt&gt;mtp_tsname&lt;/tt&gt;/NAME_MAX it returns &lt;tt&gt;-ENAMETOOLONG&lt;/tt&gt;, and that error fails the user's unlink or rename.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As a result, a user cannot delete a long-named file once a version of it is already in the trash.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="code panel" style="border-width: 1px;"&gt;&lt;div class="codeContent panelContent"&gt;
&lt;pre class="code-java"&gt;
mdd_finish_unlink(): Failed to insert object [0x20000afe1:0x22:0x0] into trash ... rc = -36
LustreError: osd_handler.c:2531:osd_object_release() ASSERTION( !(o-&amp;gt;oo_destroyed == 0 &amp;amp;&amp;amp; o-&amp;gt;oo_inode &amp;amp;&amp;amp; o-&amp;gt;oo_inode-&amp;gt;i_nlink == 0) ) failed
LBUG -&amp;gt; kernel panic (panic_on_oops=1), vm00 rebooted &lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Analysis:&lt;/b&gt; by the time the trash insert fails with -ENAMETOOLONG (the name plus the timestamp suffix is longer than NAME_MAX), the unlink transaction has already dropped the object to nlink 0. No destroy is declared on the error path, so the next &lt;tt&gt;osd_object_release()&lt;/tt&gt; hits the assertion. Any unprivileged user who can create a file with a long name can crash the MDS, so this is a DoS.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;a name="Suggestedfix"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Suggested fix&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Truncate the base name so the suffix fits, or fall back to the &lt;tt&gt;oldest&lt;/tt&gt; behavior for that object. The unlink must never fail because of the trash name.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
